      "problem": "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
